@startuml temperature
' How raw ADC counts become degrees, and where each guard sits.
' Grounded in Src/temperature.c, Inc/temperature.h, Src/adc.c, Src/main.c.

title Counts to degrees: the conversion and its guards

skinparam shadowing false

start

:ts_raw = adc_temp_read()      (channel 17)
vrefint_raw = adc_vref_read()  (channel 0);

if (calibration usable?) then (no)
  #ffdddd:blank flash, zero reference,
  or a zero-width span;
  :return TEMPERATURE_INVALID;
  stop
endif

if (readings within 12-bit scale\nand vrefint_raw non-zero?) then (no)
  #ffdddd:not a measurement;
  :return TEMPERATURE_INVALID;
  stop
endif

#ddeeff:ts_corrected =
ts_raw * VREFINT_CAL / vrefint_raw;
note right
  THE step that is easy to skip.
  ST calibrated at VDDA = 3.0 V.
  A Nucleo runs at 3.3 V, so the
  same die gives a ~10% smaller
  count. Applying the line without
  this rescale reads 30 C as -0.7 C.
end note

if (ts_corrected within bounds?) then (no)
  #ffdddd:the reference reading was
  far off, so the scale is nonsense;
  :return TEMPERATURE_INVALID;
  stop
endif

:deci = 300 + 1000 * (ts_corrected - TS_CAL1)
             / (TS_CAL2 - TS_CAL1);
note right
  Integer arithmetic only, by choice.
  A tenth of a degree is finer than
  the sensor's accuracy, so a float
  would buy precision the hardware
  does not have. Tenths is also
  exactly what the wire format wants.
end note

if (deci within -50 C .. 150 C?) then (no)
  #ffdddd:arithmetically fine,
  physically impossible;
  :return TEMPERATURE_INVALID;
  stop
endif

#ddffdd:return deci;
:telemetry formats "temp,23.5\\n";
note right
  Every red path ends in the sentinel,
  and telemetry then omits the temperature
  frame. The device reports the state change
  and stays silent about a temperature it
  could not compute. A number on the wire
  that is not a reading is worse than no
  reading at all.
end note

stop

@enduml
